      "content": "So, you're saying that \"social reintegration\" and \"societal value\" are necessary components of a utilitarian triage model. If we use that logic, it seems like the model inherently favors people who already occupy high-status or high-stability positions. \n\nLet's test the edge case of that logic. If we have two patients with identical clinical survival probabilities—say, both have a 90% chance of recovery—but one lives in an overlooked, low-income neighborhood with limited access to follow-up care, and the other lives in a gated community with high-end private healthcare access, how would your engine rank them? Would the \"stability\" of the second patient's environment make them the more \"utilitarian\" choice for maximizing long-term success?",
